1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,119
How do we know a new merchant really owns the email they typed?

2
00:00:03,119 --> 00:00:07,452
Anyone can type any address, so a sign-up alone proves nothing.

3
00:00:07,452 --> 00:00:13,369
The contract adds three calls: register, verify the email, and resend the code.

4
00:00:13,369 --> 00:00:18,678
Registering saves the merchant as pending, and refuses an email that is already taken.

5
00:00:18,678 --> 00:00:24,678
A one-time code then goes to that email, from a new OTP starter that any module can use.

6
00:00:24,678 --> 00:00:31,488
Redis keeps only a hash of the code, with a configured lifetime, so even a copy of Redis never reveals it.

7
00:00:31,488 --> 00:00:37,440
The email goes out through the notification service, and locally it lands in Mailpit, where we can read it.

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:42,178
The merchant sends the code back, and only then is the email marked as verified.

9
00:00:42,178 --> 00:00:46,392
Wrong codes count against a configured limit, and too many lock the code.

10
00:00:46,392 --> 00:00:49,833
Sends are limited too, so nobody can flood an inbox.

11
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:56,857
Every failure has its own stable code: email taken, code invalid, code expired, or too many attempts.

12
00:00:56,857 --> 00:01:02,773
Under load, about five thousand merchants registered and verified their email in thirty seconds.

13
00:01:02,773 --> 00:01:07,226
Next, the merchant moves through a lifecycle, from pending to active.

